<div class="coverage-note">
<p><strong>Not a registry dump.</strong> An item/block only gets a page here if it's reachable from at
least one of: a recipe (input or output), a block/creature drop table, a worldgen spawn rule (ore
deposit, crop/flower patch, tree, fruit tree), a trader's buy/sell list, an NPC dialogue reward, or
a recipe ingredient's own tool-tag (e.g. <code>tool-hammer</code>). An item that exists in the raw
game files but satisfies none of those (pure creative-mode novelties, debug items, etc.) will not
appear.</p>
</div>

<p>
<code>apps/vs-wiki/src/ingest/crossref.ts</code> builds the base index by walking every normalized recipe
(input/output codes) and every flattened loot entry (drop codes). Six later passes in
<code>apps/vs-wiki/src/ingest/index.ts</code> then widen it, each via the same
<code>ensureSpawnTarget(domain, code, kind)</code> synthesis -- "give this code a page even
though the recipe/loot walk alone never would have":
</p>
<ul>
  <li>ore/gem/rock deposits (<code>models/deposits.ts</code>) -- a block with no recipe and no drop table still needs a page to answer "where do I find this"</li>
  <li>crop/flower/mushroom spawn patches (<code>models/blockpatches.ts</code>)</li>
  <li>tree and fruit-tree spawn bands (<code>models/trees.ts</code>)</li>
  <li>block-drop-only blocks (break a block, get an item, with no recipe producing that block)</li>
  <li>tagged tools (<code>attachItemMetadata</code> in <code>crossref.ts</code> -- only ~23 files in the whole game declare a <code>tags</code> array at all)</li>
  <li>trader-only and NPC-dialogue-reward-only items (nothing else references them, but a trade listing or a reward still deserves a page)</li>
  <li>anything matched by <code>vs-manifest.json</code>'s <code>includeItems</code> globs (<code>includeManifestItems</code> in <code>crossref.ts</code>) -- a hand-picked list for things no rule reaches at all, like static translocators</li>
</ul>
<p>
Paintings, tapestries, and clothing are a deliberate, separate exception: they never join this
index at all, since they're pure decor with no recipe/drop/worldgen tie -- they get their own
dedicated catalogs instead (see their own source pages).
</p>
<h3>Descriptions and C# source links</h3>
<p>
Once the index is complete, <code>attachDescriptionsAndCodeRefs</code> in <code>crossref.ts</code>
gives every entry its <code>itemdesc-</code>/<code>blockdesc-</code> lang description, when the game
has one. It also records the C# names its own JSON definition points at: <code>class</code>,
<code>entityClass</code>, <code>behaviors[].name</code> and <code>entityBehaviors[].name</code>,
including their <code>...ByType</code> variants. Each name is looked up in
<code>apps/vs-wiki/src/ingest/csharpClassMap.json</code>, a committed name-to-file mapping. It is generated by
hand with <code>apps/vs-wiki/scripts/map-csharp-classes.ts</code> from a local clone of
<a href="https://github.com/anegostudios/vssurvivalmod">vssurvivalmod</a>, by matching each
<code>api.Register...Class("Name", typeof(Type))</code> call to the file declaring
<code>Type</code>. The C# source itself is never part of the build. Names implemented outside
vssurvivalmod (VSEssentials, the engine, mod DLLs) are listed without a link.
</p>

<h3>Random-stack placeholders</h3>
<p>
An <code>ItemStackRandomizer</code> item (<code>stackrandomizer-ore</code>, <code>stackrandomizer-clutter-scribe</code>,
&hellip;) is what a world-generated chest or vessel actually holds in a schematic; the game replaces it
with one random stack when the container is generated. <code>extractStackRandomizerPools</code> in
<code>models/loot.ts</code> reads each variant's <code>stacks</code> pool (glob-keyed
<code>attributesByType</code>, like every other <code>...ByType</code> field). In
<code>index.ts</code> every pool owner is added to the item index with its results, each normalized to
a 0&ndash;1 chance by the pool's total weight (the same normalization <code>ItemStackRandomizer.OnLoaded</code>
does), and every result gets a reverse "Can be generated by" link. A stack's own <code>attributes</code> are kept,
because for the generic <code>clutter</code> block they are what decide <em>which</em> clutter it is. The
placeholders sit in a schematic's <code>ItemCodes</code> dictionary, so "Found in structures" also reads
that, not just <code>BlockCodes</code>. A placed randomizer can additionally carry its own
<code>totalChance</code> (a chance of producing nothing); that is per-instance data and isn't modelled.
</p>

<h3>Items linked from the handbook</h3>
<p>
Guide, dialogue and class text links to items with <code>handbook://item-&lt;code&gt;</code> or
<code>block-&lt;code&gt;</code> (optionally <code>domain:code</code>). Each such link is rewritten to its
<code>/items/</code> page, and <code>index.ts</code> then makes sure every referenced item or block that really
exists has a page, even if no recipe, drop or spawn rule reaches it (muddy gravel, bony soil, pit kilns).
</p>

<h3>3D model</h3>
<p>
An item whose JSON names a shape (<code>shape</code>, or the best <code>shapeByType</code> match, with
<code>{group}</code> placeholders filled in) gets a rotatable preview of that file from <code>shapes/item/**</code>.
The shape is a tree of boxes; each element's rotation about its <code>rotationOrigin</code> is applied, and a child is positioned relative to its parent and inherits that rotation, by
its children. It is untextured -- the dedicated-server download ships no textures -- so each texture name gets a
placeholder color. A model's animations, where it has any, can be played from the Animation picker above the view. Armor and clothing shapes only describe the pieces, each attached to a named bone of the player's body; those are placed on the body's rest pose (the body itself isn't drawn). Blocks have no preview yet, and wildcard shapes (a random pick among several files) are skipped.
</p>

<h3>Combat, tool and armor stats</h3>
<p>
Weapons, tools and ammunition show the same numbers as the <a href="../weapons/">Weapons &amp; Tools</a> table (durability, attack power,
reach, damage, attack tier, tool tier, mining speed); armor and clothing show their protection, durability, warmth and stat modifiers (see
the Clothing source page for how they are read). Armor pages also list the "... repair" recipes that produce them.
</p>
<h3>Links that always work</h3>
<p>
A recipe or drop naming something the game doesn't define as an item or block on its own (abstract codes like <code>dough</code> or
<code>fruit</code>) is shown as plain text, and a wildcard like <code>plank-*</code> links to a search only when that search finds a row.
Run <code>npm run check-links</code> after a build to verify every internal link and search link.
</p>
